  <div class="container">
    
    <!-- ==========================================
         HOMEWORK 1: RIEMANN VS LEBESGUE 
         ========================================== -->
    <h1>Homework 1: Riemann and Lebesgue Integration</h1>
    
    <section class="text-content">
      <h2>1. Theoretical Background</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong class="highlight">Riemann Integral</strong> computes the area under a curve by partitioning the <em>domain</em> (the X-axis) into small subintervals and summing the area of vertical rectangles: <em>&sum; f(x<sub>i</sub>) &Delta;x</em>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong class="highlight">Lebesgue Integral</strong> flips this perspective. It partitions the <em>range</em> (the Y-axis) into subsets and measures the size (measure <em>&mu;</em>) of their pre-images in the domain: <em>&sum; y<sub>i</sub> &mu;(f<sup> -1</sup>(y<sub>i</sub>))</em>. This makes it far more robust for discontinuous or "pathological" functions.
      </p>
      
      <h3>Pathological Functions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong class="highlight">Dirichlet Function:</strong> <em>f(x) = 1</em> if <em>x</em> is rational, <em>0</em> if irrational. The Riemann integral is undefined because every subinterval contains both rationals and irrationals, causing the upper and lower sums to diverge (1 and 0). The Lebesgue integral trivially evaluates to <em>0</em> because the measure of rational numbers in [0,1] is 0 (countably infinite), so <em>1 &times; 0 + 0 &times; 1 = 0</em>.</li>
        <li><strong class="highlight">Thomae's Function:</strong> Riemann integrable (integral is 0) because discontinuities (rationals) form a set of measure zero, but Lebesgue handles it more naturally using measure theory.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="text-content">
      <h2>2. Numerical Implementation & Comparison</h2>
      <p>
        Below is a numerical simulation. The Riemann approach iterates over <em>x</em> and accumulates areas. The pseudo-Lebesgue numerical approach evaluates <em>f(x)</em>, groups identical/similar <em>y</em> values into bins (simulating the pre-image measure), and sums <em>y &times; measure(Y-bin)</em>.
      </p>

      <div class="interactive-panel">
        <div class="input-group">
          <label for="func-select">Select Function f(x):</label>
          <select id="func-select">
            <option value="sin">f(x) = sin(x), x &isin; [0, &pi;]</option>
            <option value="x2">f(x) = x&sup2;, x &isin; [0, 1]</option>
            <option value="step">f(x) = 1 if x < 0.5 else 3, x &isin; [0, 1]</option>
            <option value="abs">f(x) = |x - 0.5|, x &isin; [0, 1]</option>
            <option value="sinc">f(x) = sin(x)/x, x &isin; [0, &pi;]</option>
            <option value="invsqrt">f(x) = 1 / &radic;x, x &isin; (0, 1]</option>
