    <h1>Homework 2: Numerical Computation of the Normal CDF</h1>

    <section class="text-content">
      <h2>1. Liouville's Theorem and the Normal CDF</h2>
      <p>
        The Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) of the standard normal distribution is defined as: <br>
        <span class="highlight">&Phi;(z) = (1 / &radic;(2&pi;)) &int;<sub>-&infin;</sub><sup>z</sup> e<sup>-t&sup2;/2</sup> dt</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        By <strong class="highlight">Liouville's Theorem</strong> (in differential algebra), the antiderivative of <em>e<sup>-t&sup2;</sup></em> cannot be expressed in terms of elementary functions (polynomials, exponentials, logarithms, and trigonometric functions). Therefore, &Phi;(z) cannot be evaluated analytically using standard calculus techniques.
      </p>
      <p>
        To bypass this, mathematics introduces special functions, specifically the <strong class="highlight">Error Function (erf)</strong>. The exact analytical representation becomes: <br>
        <span class="highlight">&Phi;(z) = 0.5 &times; [1 + erf(z / &radic;2)]</span><br>
        However, since <code>erf(x)</code> is itself a special function without an elementary closed form, we must rely on numerical methods (like Taylor Series or Numerical Integration) for practical computer implementation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="text-content">
      <h2>2. Numerical Implementation</h2>
      <p>
        The engine below computes the CDF using three distinct numerical approaches and compares computational efficiency and accuracy against JavaScript's built-in mathematical limits.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong class="highlight">Numerical Integration (Simpson's Rule):</strong> Integrates the PDF from <em>-8</em> (a numerical approximation of -&infin;) to <em>z</em>.</li>
        <li><strong class="highlight">Taylor Series (erf expansion):</strong> Uses the power series of the error function. Highly accurate for small <em>z</em>, but suffers from floating-point overflow for large <em>z</em>.</li>
        <li><strong class="highlight">Abramowitz and Stegun Approximation:</strong> A trusted, highly optimized numerical approximation formula for <code>erf</code> widely used in engineering.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="interactive-panel">
        <div class="input-group">
          <label for="z-input">Input z-value (e.g., 0, 1.96, -2.5):</label>
          <input type="number" id="z-input" value="1.96" step="0.01">
        </div>
        
        <button class="btn-run" id="btn-run-cdf">Compute Normal CDF</button>
        
        <div class="results-box" id="cdf-results">
          Waiting for execution...
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

